{
  "result": "At the edge of every city, there is usually a place where the pavement gives up. Weeds push through cracks, fences lean, and the sound of traffic becomes less an order than a weather system. I like these margins because they refuse to perform importance. No one plans a photograph there, yet the world continues its patient experiments.\n\nA bottle catches rainwater and turns a small sky upside down. A sparrow discovers breadcrumbs beneath a bench. Rust spreads across a gate with the slow confidence of a continent. Even discarded things seem to possess a second life when no one is watching.\n\nPerhaps that is why walking feels clarifying. The mind, crowded with appointments, messages, and imagined arguments, begins to imitate the path: one step, then another, without demanding a conclusion. A question can remain a question for several blocks. A regret can soften when carried past a flowering hedge.\n\nWe are often told to make our lives efficient, visible, measurable. But some of the best hours have no evidence except tired shoes and a changed attention. They vanish almost immediately, like steam from a cup held outside in winter.\n\nStill, their absence is not emptiness. It is a kind of compost, dark and quiet, feeding decisions we will not recognize until later. The next morning, we may answer more gently, notice a neighbor, or leave room for uncertainty. That is enough. A life need not always announce its transformations. Sometimes it simply grows, unnoticed, beside the road, after rain.",
